    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "The build is green: `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` and `BUILD SUCCESS`.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented idempotent create for `POST /api/owners`: a repeat carrying an already-seen `Idempotency-Key` header now returns the originally created owner with **200**, instead of creating a duplicate.\n\nSince this project serves REST through OfficeFloor function-injection pipelines (not `@RestController`), I did it by extending the pipeline, not with a Spring interceptor:\n\n**New files** (in `rest/function/owner/`):\n- `IdempotencyStore.java` \u2014 a `@Component` singleton mapping seen key \u2192 created owner id (survives across requests; keys the pipeline records after save).\n- `CheckIdempotencyKey.java` \u2014 new **entry step**. Reads the `Idempotency-Key` header via `@HttpHeaderParameter`; if the key was already used, it responds with the original owner (200) and stops. Otherwise it takes a `@Flow(\"proceed\")` branch into the existing pipeline.\n- `RecordIdempotencyKey.java` \u2014 runs right after `SaveOwner`, recording key \u2192 `owner.getId()`.\n\n**YAML change** (`officefloor/rest/api/owners.POST.yml`):\n- Added `checkIdempotency` as the first (entry) function, with `outputs: { proceed: normalizeAddress }`.\n- Inserted `recordIdempotency` between `save` and `audit`.\n\nKey correctness points I verified against the framework internals before writing code:\n- The **first** YAML function key is the pipeline entry point (`ComposeConfiguration` captures the first `@JsonAnySetter` function as `start`), so `checkIdempotency` runs first.\n- `@HttpHeaderParameter` resolves to `null` when the header is absent (`HttpArgumentManagedObjectSource` defaults the value to `null`), so all existing **key-less** creates are unaffected \u2014 confirmed by the suite staying green.\n- `@Flow(\"proceed\")` on a single-method functional interface creates a \"proceed\" branch that, when not called, short-circuits the pipeline \u2014 exactly the create-vs-replay fork needed.\n- The header is read (not consumed like the body), so both `checkIdempotency` and `recordIdempotency` can read it independently; only `NormalizeAddress` binds `@RequestBody`, preserving the one-body-read rule.\n\nThe idempotent replay responds 200 (plain `ObjectResponse<OwnerDto>`) vs. the normal 201 (`ResponseEntity.created(...)`), matching the spec.",
